The archive should make valuable understanding recoverable even after significant time has passed.

Archive Philosophy

The ecosystem does not treat archives as passive repositories.

Archives are active infrastructure.

Their role is not merely remembering the past.

Their role is helping the future inherit understanding.

The value of an archive is measured not by how much it stores, but by how effectively it supports continuity.

Archival Principles
Preservation

Valuable records should remain protected from unnecessary loss.

Preservation exists to support continuity.

Accessibility

Preserved records should remain discoverable.

Knowledge that cannot be found is functionally lost.

Recoverability

Records should remain retrievable even after significant time has passed.

Continuity

Archives should strengthen long-term understanding across generations.

Integrity

Records should remain as accurate and authentic as reasonably possible.

Reconstructability

The archive should support reconstruction whenever complete preservation becomes impossible.

