Archive Layers

The archive operates through multiple layers:

Layer 1 — Records

Raw preservation.

Examples:

notes,
files,
conversations,
revisions.
Layer 2 — Documentation

Structured information.

Examples:

manuals,
procedures,
specifications,
standards.
Layer 3 — Knowledge

Validated understanding.

Examples:

frameworks,
methodologies,
architectures.
Layer 4 — Heritage

Knowledge judged worthy of inheritance.

Examples:

principles,
doctrines,
foundational lessons.
Layer 5 — Continuity

The ability of future generations to rebuild understanding from preserved material.

This is the highest archival objective.

Retrieval Principle

Preservation alone is insufficient.

Archives should support:

retrieval,
interpretation,
reconstruction,
teaching,
and practical use.

Knowledge that cannot be recovered provides limited value.

Context Preservation

Information without context may become misleading.

Archives should preserve:

origin,
purpose,
assumptions,
relationships,
historical conditions,
constraints,
and intended meaning

whenever practical.

Context protects understanding.

Reconstruction Principle

Perfect preservation is impossible.

Therefore archives should maximize reconstructability.

If complete knowledge is lost, future generations should still possess enough evidence to rebuild understanding.

The ecosystem values:

reasoning traces,
design decisions,
architectural explanations,
historical records,
and protocol relationships

because they improve reconstruction.

Versioning

Understanding evolves.

Archives should preserve meaningful versions whenever appropriate.

Versioning supports:

historical reconstruction,
development tracking,
revision analysis,
and institutional memory.

The objective is not preserving every change.

The objective is preserving meaningful evolution.

Archive Stewardship

Archives require active stewardship.

Responsibilities include:

maintenance,
organization,
preservation,
migration,
verification,
and continuity management.

Archives deteriorate when neglected.

Stewardship prevents institutional forgetting.

Archival Risks

Common archival risks include:

Loss

Records disappear.

Corruption

Records become altered or damaged.

Fragmentation

Knowledge becomes scattered.

Obsolescence

Formats become unusable.

Misinterpretation

Context disappears.

Neglect

Maintenance stops.

Overaccumulation

The archive becomes so large that valuable understanding becomes difficult to locate.

Preservation without organization eventually creates noise.

Archive Quality Principle

The value of an archive is determined by:

Recoverability
+
Accessibility
+
Integrity
+
Context
+
Continuity

rather than storage volume alone.

More information does not automatically create a better archive.

